  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier and cooler weather for the next few days.\n\nRain showers will persist this morning for the western half of the \nforecast area as a mid-level trough squeezes through two mid-level \nhighs to its north and southwest. The system looks to be to our \nsouth by 15Z and precipitation chances are projected to tank by \nthen. Cloud cover will still be extensive but will gradually clear \nout by Thursday night.\n\nThe mid-level trough will intensify and become a nor'easter as it \nheads into the Atlantic and slowly make its way northeast. With a \n500mb ridge over the Texas/Louisiana border, the wind profile will \nbe northerly for our region and colder and drier conditions will \narrive. Though there will be some wind, the clear skies may \nallow some large temperature ranges. Early morning lows in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s are possible west of the Blue Ridge \nFriday and Saturday morning. Highs during these days will be in \nthe upper 60s to mid-70s west of the Blue Ridge and in the upper\n70s to lower 80s east of the Blue Ridge. Dew points for the \nnext several days will dwell in the 40s and 50s. The nor'easter \nis not expected to impact the area and will progresses further \nnortheast by early next week. Around the same time, the ridge to\nour southwest moves towards Mexico. This transitions the wind \ndirection to a more westerly pattern and allows temperatures to \ngradually warm up again by the middle of next week.",
